<section class="col-xs-12 col-sm-12 col-md-12 col-lg-9 col-xl-9" id="main">
<div class="html" id="tag">
<h3 class="tex-subsection" id="subsection:infinity-of-infinity"><span data-tag="0208">5.5.3</span> The $\infty $-Category of $\infty $-Categories</h3>
<p>
Let $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$ denote the homotopy category of (small) $\infty $-categories (Construction <a data-tag="01DR" href="/tag/01DR">4.5.1.1</a>). Recall that the objects of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$ are (small) $\infty $-categories, and a morphism from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ in $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$ is an isomorphism class of functors from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. In this section, we show that $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$ can be realized as the homotopy category of an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$, which we will refer to as <em>the $\infty $-category of $\infty $-categories</em>. Proceeding as in §<a data-tag="01YX" href="/tag/01YX">3.1.6</a>, we will realize $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ as the homotopy coherent nerve of a simplicial category. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-construction" id="0209">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0209">Construction <span data-tag="0209">5.5.3.1</span> <span class="named">(The $\infty $-Category of $\infty $-Categories)</span>.</a> We define a simplicial category $\operatorname{QCat}$ as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The objects of $\operatorname{QCat}$ are (small) $\infty $-categories. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are $\infty $-categories, then the simplicial set $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{QCat}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})_{\bullet }$ is the core $\operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$ of the functor $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ are $\infty $-categories, then the composition law 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \circ : \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{QCat}}(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}})_{\bullet } \times \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{QCat}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})_{\bullet } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{QCat}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}})_{\bullet } \]
</div>
<p>
 is induced by the composition map $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}) \times \operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}})$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> We let $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ denote the homotopy coherent nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat})$. We will refer to $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ as the <em>$\infty $-category of $\infty $-categories</em>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020A">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020A">Remark <span data-tag="020A">5.5.3.2</span>.</a> Many authors use the term <em>quasicategory</em> for what we refer to as an $\infty $-category (see Remark <a data-tag="003B" href="/tag/003B">1.4.0.2</a>); the notations of Construction <a data-tag="0209" href="/tag/0209">5.5.3.1</a> reflect this alternative terminology. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="020B">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020B">Proposition <span data-tag="020B">5.5.3.3</span>.</a> The simplicial set $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is an $\infty $-category. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      For every pair of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the core $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$ is a Kan complex (Corollary <a data-tag="01H1" href="/tag/01H1">4.4.3.11</a>). It follows that the simplicial category $\operatorname{QCat}$ of Construction <a data-tag="0209" href="/tag/0209">5.5.3.1</a> is locally Kan, so its homotopy coherent nerve $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat})$ is an $\infty $-category by virtue of Theorem <a data-tag="00LJ" href="/tag/00LJ">2.4.5.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020C">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020C">Remark <span data-tag="020C">5.5.3.4</span>.</a> The low-dimensional simplices of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ are simple to describe: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
An object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is a (small) $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$, then a morphism from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is a functor $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A $2$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ can be identified with a diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\ar [dr]^{G} \ar@ {=&gt;}[]+&lt;0pt,-15pt&gt;;+&lt;0pt,-60pt&gt;^-{\mu }_-{\sim } &amp;  \\ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [ur]^{F} \ar [rr]_{H} &amp;  &amp;  \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}} \]
</div>
<p>
 where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, and $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ are (small) $\infty $-categories, $F$, $G$, and $H$ are functors, and $\mu : G \circ F \rightarrow H$ is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{E}})$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020F">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020F">Remark <span data-tag="020F">5.5.3.5</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be $\infty $-categories. Then Remark <a data-tag="01LF" href="/tag/01LF">4.6.7.6</a> supplies a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes $\phi : \operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$. Beware that this homotopy equivalence is generally not an isomorphism. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020D">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020D">Remark <span data-tag="020D">5.5.3.6</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}}$ denote the homotopy category of $\infty $-categories (Construction <a data-tag="01DR" href="/tag/01DR">4.5.1.1</a>), which we view as an $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$-enriched category (see Remark <a data-tag="02L1" href="/tag/02L1">3.1.5.12</a>). Applying Proposition <a data-tag="00M4" href="/tag/00M4">2.4.6.9</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="02LP" href="/tag/02LP">4.6.8.20</a>, we obtain a canonical isomorphism of $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$-enriched categories $\Phi : \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{QCat}} \xrightarrow {\sim } \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}}$, which is given on objects by the construction $\Phi (\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and on morphism spaces by the homotopy equivalences </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{QCat}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})_{\bullet } = \operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}) \]
</div>
<p> of Remark <a data-tag="020F" href="/tag/020F">5.5.3.5</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020E">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020E">Remark <span data-tag="020E">5.5.3.7</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="01DY" href="/tag/01DY">4.5.1.10</a>) if and only if it is an <em>isomorphism</em> in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020G">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020G">Remark <span data-tag="020G">5.5.3.8</span> <span class="named">(Comparison with Kan Complexes)</span>.</a> Every Kan complex is an $\infty $-category (Example <a data-tag="003C" href="/tag/003C">1.4.0.3</a>). Moreover, if $X$ and $Y$ are Kan complexes, then the simplicial set $\operatorname{Fun}(X,Y)$ is also a Kan complex (Corollary <a data-tag="00TN" href="/tag/00TN">3.1.3.4</a>), and therefore coincides with its core $\operatorname{Fun}(X,Y)^{\simeq }$. It follows that we can regard the simplicial category $\operatorname{Kan}$ of Construction <a data-tag="00TZ" href="/tag/00TZ">3.1.6.1</a> as a full simplicial subcategory of $\operatorname{QCat}$. Passing to homotopy coherent nerves, we deduce that the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{Kan})$ is the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat})$ spanned by the Kan complexes. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="05Q1">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05Q1">Remark <span data-tag="05Q1">5.5.3.9</span> <span class="named">(Functoriality of the Core)</span>.</a> For every $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq }$ denote the core of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Construction <a data-tag="01D0" href="/tag/01D0">4.4.3.1</a>). The construction $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq }$ determines a simplicially enriched functor $\operatorname{QCat}\rightarrow \operatorname{Kan}$. Passing to homotopy coherent nerves, we obtain a functor of $\infty $-categories </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}\quad \quad \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq }. \]
</div>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="020H">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/020H">Remark <span data-tag="020H">5.5.3.10</span> <span class="named">(Comparison with Categories)</span>.</a> Let $\mathbf{Cat}$ denote the strict bicategory of small categories (Example <a data-tag="007M" href="/tag/007M">2.2.0.4</a>), let $\operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} )$ denote its pith (Construction <a data-tag="00AL" href="/tag/00AL">2.2.8.9</a>), and let us abuse notation by identifying $\operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} )$ with the simplicial category described in Example <a data-tag="00KA" href="/tag/00KA">2.4.2.8</a>. Concretely, this simplicial category can be described as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The objects of $\operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} )$ are small categories. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ are objects of $\operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} )$, then the simplicial set $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} )}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})_{\bullet }$ is the nerve of the groupoid $\operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$ whose objects are functors from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ and whose morphisms are natural isomorphisms. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> By virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="0062" href="/tag/0062">1.5.3.3</a>, the construction $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\mapsto \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ determines a fully faithful embedding of simplicial categories $\operatorname{Pith}(\mathbf{Cat} ) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{QCat}$. Passing to homotopy coherent nerves (and invoking Example <a data-tag="00KY" href="/tag/00KY">2.4.3.11</a>), we obtain a functor of $\infty $-categories $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}( \operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} ) ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. Unwinding the definitions, we see that this functor induces an <em>isomorphism</em> from the Duskin nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{D}}( \operatorname{Pith}( \mathbf{Cat} ) )$ to the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ spanned by those $\infty $-categories of the form $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an ordinary category. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="03UT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UT">Variant <span data-tag="03UT">5.5.3.11</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable cardinal. We let $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ denote the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ spanned by the $\infty $-categories which are $\kappa $-small. We will refer to $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ as the <em>$\infty $-category of $\kappa $-small $\infty $-categories</em>. </p>
<p>If $\kappa $ is any infinite cardinal, we let $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{\leq \kappa }$ denote the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa ^{+} }$, where $\kappa ^{+}$ denotes the successor of $\kappa $. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="03UU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UU">Remark <span data-tag="03UU">5.5.3.12</span> <span class="named">(Set-Theoretic Conventions)</span>.</a> By definition, the objects of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat})$ are <em>small</em> $\infty $-categories. According to the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, this means that we restrict our attention to essentially $ \Omega $-small $\infty $-categories where $ \Omega $ is some fixed uncountable strongly inaccessible cardinal. In this case, the definitions given in Variant <a data-tag="03UT" href="/tag/03UT">5.5.3.11</a> are appropriate only for uncountable cardinals $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $. More generally, if $\kappa $ is an arbitrary uncountable cardinal, we define $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ to be the homotopy coherent nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat}_{&lt; \kappa } )$, where $\operatorname{QCat}_{&lt; \kappa }$ denotes the (simplicially enriched) category of $\kappa $-small $\infty $-categories. We then have three cases: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ is a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa =  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ coincides with $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(c)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa &gt;  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> To simplify the exposition, we will often implicitly assume that we are in case $(a)$, as suggested in Variant <a data-tag="03UT" href="/tag/03UT">5.5.3.11</a>. However, it will be convenient to also allow case $(c)$ when working with $\infty $-categories which are not necessarily small (such as $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ itself). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-variant" id="03UV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UV">Variant <span data-tag="03UV">5.5.3.13</span>.</a> Let $\kappa $ be an uncountable cardinal. We let $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ denote the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ spanned by the $\kappa $-small Kan complexes, which we also regard as a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$. If $\kappa $ is any infinite cardinal, we let $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{\leq \kappa }$ denote the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa ^{+} }$, where $\kappa ^{+}$ denotes the successor of $\kappa $. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="03UW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UW">Remark <span data-tag="03UW">5.5.3.14</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, where $\lambda $ has exponential cofinality $\geq \kappa $ (Definition <a data-tag="03RT" href="/tag/03RT">4.9.3.16</a>). Then the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ is locally $\lambda $-small. This follows by combining Remarks <a data-tag="020F" href="/tag/020F">5.5.3.5</a> and <a data-tag="03ST" href="/tag/03ST">4.9.5.13</a>. It follows that the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa } \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ is also locally $\lambda $-small. If $\kappa &lt; \lambda $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ are essentially $\lambda $-small (Remark <a data-tag="0614" href="/tag/0614">4.9.4.22</a>). </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="06T5">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T5">Proposition <span data-tag="06T5">5.5.3.15</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between small $\infty $-categories and let $n$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $n$-truncated when viewed as a morphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (see Definition <a data-tag="05F9" href="/tag/05F9">4.7.2.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every small $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{K}}$, composition with $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{K}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{K}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every simplicial set $K$, composition with $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ induces $n$-truncated morphisms of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(5)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$. Moreover, for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the induced map of morphism spaces $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), F(Y) )$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (2)$ follows from Remark <a data-tag="020F" href="/tag/020F">5.5.3.5</a> and the implications $(3) \Rightarrow (2) \Rightarrow (4)$ are trivial. We next show that $(4)$ implies $(3)$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $F$ is an isofibration. By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="02VM" href="/tag/02VM">4.5.3.24</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that $F$ is an isofibration of $\infty $-categories. Then, for every simplicial set $K$, composition with $F$ induces a Kan fibration $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. Using Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a>, we can reformulate condition $(3)$ as follows: 
    <p>
<dl>
<dt>$(3')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every diagram $q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the Kan complex $\{ q\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>
       Let us now regard the diagram $q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as fixed. For every simplicial set $L$ equipped with a map $L \rightarrow K$, let $T(L)$ denote the Kan complex $\{  q|_{L} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$. Let us say that $L$ is <em>good</em> if $T(L)$ is $n$-truncated. Assuming that condition $(4)$ is satisfied, we will show that every simplicial set $L \in (\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$ is good. The proof proceeds in several steps: 
    <p>
<ul>
<li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
The construction $L \mapsto T(L)$ carries colimits (in the category of simplicial sets equipped with a map to $K$) to limits (in the category of simplicial sets). This follows from the characterization of isomorphisms in $\operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ supplied by Theorem <a data-tag="01DK" href="/tag/01DK">4.4.4.4</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
The collection of good objects of $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$ is closed under coproducts. This follows from $(a)$, since the collection of $n$-truncated Kan complexes is closed under products (Remark <a data-tag="054U" href="/tag/054U">3.5.7.6</a>). 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(c)"><p>
If $L \hookrightarrow L'$ is a monomorphism of simplicial sets equipped with a map to $K$, then the restriction map $T(L') \rightarrow T(L')$ is a Kan fibration between Kan complexes. This follows from Propositions <a data-tag="01F1" href="/tag/01F1">4.4.5.1</a> and <a data-tag="01EZ" href="/tag/01EZ">4.4.3.7</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(d)"><p>
Suppose we are given a pushout diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ L_{01} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  L_0 \ar [d] \\ L_1 \ar [r] &amp;  L } \]
</div>
<p>
 in the category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$, where the horizontal maps are monomorphisms. It follows from $(a)$ and $(c)$ that the diagram of Kan complexes 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ T( L_{01} ) &amp;  T(L_0) \ar [l] \\ T(L_1) \ar [u] &amp;  T(L) \ar [u] \ar [l] } \]
</div>
<p>
 is a pullback square in which the horizontal maps are Kan fibrations, and therefore a homotopy pullback square (Example <a data-tag="010S" href="/tag/010S">3.4.1.4</a>). Consequently, if the simplicial sets $L_0$, $L_1$, and $L_{01}$ are good, then $L$ is also good (Corollary <a data-tag="06QK" href="/tag/06QK">3.5.9.14</a>). 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(e)"><p>
The collection of good simplicial sets is closed under the formation of retracts (in the category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$). See Proposition <a data-tag="06QJ" href="/tag/06QJ">3.5.7.7</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(f)"><p>
Let $L \rightarrow K$ be a morphism of simplicial sets where $L$ has dimension $\leq m$, for some integer $m$; we claim that $L$ is good. The proof proceeds by induction on $m$. Using $(b)$, $(d)$, and Proposition <a data-tag="001B" href="/tag/001B">1.1.4.12</a>, we can reduce to the case where $L = \Delta ^ m$ is a standard $m$-simplex. If $m =1$, the desired result follows our hypothesis that the functor $F$ satisfies $(4)$. The case $m = 0$ then follows from $(e)$. For $m \geq 2$, the inclusion $\operatorname{Spine}[m] \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ m$ is inner anodyne (Example <a data-tag="00JA" href="/tag/00JA">1.5.7.7</a>) and therefore induces a trivial Kan fibration $T( \Delta ^ m ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{Spine}[m] )$. It will therefore suffice to show that $\operatorname{Spine}[m]$ is good, which follows from our inductive hypothesis. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(g)"><p>
Let $L$ be any simplicial set equipped with a map to $K$. Using $(a)$, we see that the Kan complex $T(L)$ can be realized as the inverse limit of a tower 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{3}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{2}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{1}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_0(L) ), \]
</div>
<p>
 where the transition maps are Kan fibrations by virtue of $(c)$. It follows from $(f)$ that each of the Kan complexes $T( \operatorname{sk}_{m}(L) )$ is $n$-truncated. Applying Corollary <a data-tag="06QN" href="/tag/06QN">3.5.9.26</a>, we conclude that $T(L)$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
      We now complete the proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06T5" href="/tag/06T5">5.5.3.15</a> by showing that conditions $(4)$ and $(5)$ are equivalent. As above, we may assume that $F$ is an isofibration of $\infty $-categories, and therefore induces a Kan fibration $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( X, Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}(F(X), F(Y) )$ for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="01P8" href="/tag/01P8">4.6.1.22</a>). Moreover, we may also assume that the map of cores $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated (since this is part of assumption $(5)$, and follows from $(4)$ by the argument given above). Under these assumptions, we can use Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> to reformulate conditions $(4)$ and $(5)$ as follows: 
    <p>
<dl>
<dt>$(4')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the Kan complex $T( \overline{f} ) = \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(5')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ and every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $F(X) = \overline{X}$ and $F(Y) = \overline{Y}$, the Kan complex 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{ \overline{f} } = \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( \overline{X}, \overline{Y} ) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \]
</div>
<p>
 is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>
      Fix a morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq }$ denote the fiber of the Kan fibration $F^{\simeq }: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$ over $\overline{X}$, and define $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq }$ similarly. Since $F^{\simeq }$ is an $n$-truncated Kan fibration, the Kan complexes $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq }$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq }$ are $n$-truncated (Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a>). It follows that the Kan complex $T( \overline{f} )$ is $n$-truncated if and only if the restriction map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  T( \overline{f} ) \xrightarrow {\theta } \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \simeq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq } \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq } \]
</div>
<p>
       is $n$-truncated (Proposition <a data-tag="056C" href="/tag/056C">3.5.9.13</a>). It follows from Propositions <a data-tag="01F1" href="/tag/01F1">4.4.5.1</a> and <a data-tag="01EZ" href="/tag/01EZ">4.4.3.7</a> that $\theta $ is a Kan fibration. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> again, we see that this condition is satisfied if and only if each fiber of $\theta $ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex. The equivalence of $(4')$ and $(5')$ now follows by allowing the morphism $\overline{f}$ to vary. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></p></p></p></p></p></article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06T6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T6">Corollary <span data-tag="06T6">5.5.3.16</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between small $\infty $-categories. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is a monomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the functor $F$ induces a homotopy equivalence from $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ to a summand of $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), F(Y) )$ which contains every isomorphism from $F(X)$ to $F(Y)$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ induces an equivalence from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to a replete subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Proposition <a data-tag="06T5" href="/tag/06T5">5.5.3.15</a> in the case $n = -1$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="04W6">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04W6">Corollary <span data-tag="04W6">5.5.3.17</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a fully faithful functor of $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is a monomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be the essential image of $F$. By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="06T6" href="/tag/06T6">5.5.3.16</a>, it will suffice to show that $F$ induces an equivalence from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$, which is a reformulation of the requirement that $F$ is fully faithful (Corollary <a data-tag="01K0" href="/tag/01K0">4.8.4.4</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-warning" id="04W7">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04W7">Warning <span data-tag="04W7">5.5.3.18</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a subcategory. Beware that, if we do not assume that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ is replete (or full), then the inclusion functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ need not be a monomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. For example, suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$ is the nerve of a category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. Then the $0$-skeleton $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 = \operatorname{sk}_{0}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ is always a subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (namely, the subcategory spanned by the identity morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$). However, the inclusion $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0 \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a monomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ if and only if every isomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an identity morphism. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="06T7">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T7">Corollary <span data-tag="06T7">5.5.3.19</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a small $\infty $-category and let $n$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated when viewed as an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $n$-truncated (Definition <a data-tag="0584" href="/tag/0584">4.7.6.1</a>) and the core $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq }$ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Apply Proposition <a data-tag="06T5" href="/tag/06T5">5.5.3.15</a> in the special case where $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}= \Delta ^0$ (see Example <a data-tag="05FP" href="/tag/05FP">4.7.2.13</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="05EV">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05EV">Remark <span data-tag="05EV">5.5.3.20</span>.</a> If $n \geq -1$, we can reformulate condition $(2)$ of Proposition <a data-tag="06T7" href="/tag/06T7">5.5.3.19</a> as follows: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(2')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ is $n$-truncated. Moreover, the summand $\operatorname{Isom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \subseteq \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ spanned by the isomorphisms from $X$ to $Y$ is $(n-1)$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> See Example <a data-tag="056J" href="/tag/056J">3.5.9.19</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-corollary" id="05EW">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05EW">Corollary <span data-tag="05EW">5.5.3.21</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a small $\infty $-category and let $n$ be an integer. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
If $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is an $n$-truncated object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EC" href="/tag/05EC">4.7.1.1</a>), then it is locally $n$-truncated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0584" href="/tag/0584">4.7.6.1</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $n \geq -1$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally $(n-1)$-truncated, then it is an $n$-truncated object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-warning" id="05EX">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05EX">Warning <span data-tag="05EX">5.5.3.22</span>.</a> In general, neither implication of Corollary <a data-tag="05EW" href="/tag/05EW">5.5.3.21</a> is reversible. See Example <a data-tag="05F3" href="/tag/05F3">5.5.3.23</a>. </p>
</article>
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-example" id="05F3">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05F3">Example <span data-tag="05F3">5.5.3.23</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ be the $\infty $-category of (small) $\infty $-categories (Construction <a data-tag="0209" href="/tag/0209">5.5.3.1</a>). Then an object $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\in \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is discrete (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="05EY" href="/tag/05EY">4.7.4.13</a>) if and only if it satisfies the following pair of conditions: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is locally discrete: that is, there exists an equivalence $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0)$, where $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$ is an ordinary category (see Corollary <a data-tag="06RE" href="/tag/06RE">4.7.4.23</a>). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_0$, the automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}(X)$ is trivial. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> Note the second condition cannot be omitted. </p>
</article>
</p>
